    <!-- Scripting Tutorial -->
    <string name="script_tutorial_dialog_title">Scripting tutorial</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page1_welcome">Welcome to scripting feature!</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page1_content">Scripts allow you to generate random cards. It\'s very useful when you need to create many similar cards</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page2_title">Here is an example</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page2_content1">Imagine you want to create a quiz about multiplication table. Normally, you would need to create around 100 cards such as «What is 2×7», «What is 8×9», etc.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page2_content2">The script feature fixes this problem. You simply need to create a single card that has this question: «What is A×B» where A and B are some random numbers</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page3_title">Change question and answer</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page3_content1">To make a card for multiplication table, you need to change it\'s question and answer to this:</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page3_question">What is {A} × {B}</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page3_content2">Our script will substitute random numbers into the letters: A, B, C, where the C will store the answer. To tell apart these variables from a normal text, we wrap them with curly braces</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page4_title">Coding time</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page4_content1">Now let\'s move into coding. Under the hood, Solocards uses a programming language called Lua.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page4_content2_part1">We use a function</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page4_content2_part2">to obtain a random number from 1 to 10 inclusive</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page4_content3">Then we multiply these numbers and assign them to C variable (which stores the answer for our card)</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page5_title">Test your card</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page5_content1">Congrats, now this card can be used to train your multiplication table skills!</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page5_content2">Click Test button in the Code Editor to generate random card.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page5_content3">By the way, also you are able to generate random options for Boolean and Option mode using script! Click Next below to show how to do that</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page6_title">Making options random</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page6_content1">To generate a random option, we just do the similar thing. In the option blank, add option {D}, where D is some random number (don\'t forget for curly braces)</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page6_content2">Then we simply modify the code:</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page6_content3">In our code, the D number is just the answer minus one.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page7_title">Making more options</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page7_content1">You can add more random options to the card. Add the option {E} and option {F} to option blank and modify the code:</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page7_content2">The variable E is always bigger than answer by 1, and the F is smaller by 2. Now, you have 3 random options!</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page8_title">Advanced stuff</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page8_content1">All basic things I discussed in this tutorial. If you wanna try some advanced stuff, you can use Lua strings, lists, conditions and loops.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page8_content2">Also, if you generate a string with multiple options separated by comma and put it into option blank, you will have multiple options (isn\'t considered in this tutorial)</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page8_content3">See the Example Quiz in the quiz menu for more examples.</string>
    <string name="script_tutorial_page8_goodluck">Good luck in scripting!</string>

